A slice of cases + a loop with `t.Run` per case:

```go
cases := []struct{ name string; in, want int }{
    {"zero", 0, 0}, {"pos", 2, 4},
}
for _, c := range cases {
    t.Run(c.name, func(t *testing.T) {
        if got := Square(c.in); got != c.want {
            t.Errorf("Square(%d)=%d, want %d", c.in, got, c.want)
        }
    })
}
```

Why: adding a case is one line; `t.Run` gives each case its own name in output and lets you run one with `-run`; failures don't stop the others. Mention `t.Helper()` for assertion helpers and `-race`/`-cover` flags.

Depend on a small **interface**, not a concrete client, and inject it. In tests pass a fake/stub that implements the interface; in prod pass the real one (the bookmarks API in Step 4 uses exactly this `Store` seam).

```go
type UserStore interface { ByID(ctx, id) (*User, error) }
// handler takes UserStore; test passes a fakeStore returning canned data
```

For HTTP, use `net/http/httptest` to spin a real test server, or inject an `http.Client` with a custom `RoundTripper`. Key principle: invert the dependency at a narrow interface so tests are fast, deterministic, and offline.
